# CV Studio rules

## Source of truth (evidence hierarchy)

1. **`data/master.yaml`** — every fact on the PDF (employers, **job titles**, dates, bullets, projects, skills).
2. **Your personal knowledge base** (optional) — a private, local, not-committed file where you keep depth/context that doesn't fit on the CV: metrics, project honesty notes, a "do not oversell" list, career-story framing. If you keep one, tell the agent where it lives (e.g. `~/.cursor/<you>-knowledge-base.md`) so it can be read alongside master.yaml. Use it when tailoring; if something is not in master or the knowledge base, do not put it on a CV.
3. **Job description** — decides **what to emphasize**, not what to invent.

**Tailoring = same person, different spotlight:** reorder and reword; hide lower-priority bullets for space; pick headline/summary angle per role. **Not** new skills, titles, employers, years, or tools because the JD asks for them.

- `data/master.example.yaml` is the committed template with placeholders (for GitHub / new users).
- `data/master.yaml` is your local CV data (gitignored). Run `npm run setup` to create it from the template.
- `data/bases/*.yaml` holds base profiles (gitignored) — one per role family you commonly target (e.g. a general one plus a couple of specialized ones). Committed templates live as `data/bases/*.example.yaml`. Inspect the ids/labels actually present locally before assuming names.
- `data/saved/*.yaml` stores job-specific variants (local, gitignored). See `data/saved/*.example.yaml` for committed examples.
- Never invent experience, skills, titles, or metrics in master or versions.
- **`roleTitleOverrides` is forbidden** for agents and BYO AI. To fix a title, edit **master** only after the user confirms it matches reality (e.g. HR record).
- `internalPeriod` on a role is for interview context only — it is not shown on the PDF.
- Preview **Edit mode** writes to the active base/saved YAML only — never to `master.yaml` from the UI.

## Voice & content rules
- **No em dashes (`—`)** in CV copy (headline, summary, bullets, projects, education). They render as an overly wide dash in PDF. Use a comma, colon, semicolon, or a regular hyphen `-` instead (e.g. `Engineer - Platform`, not `Engineer — Platform`).
- **No self-disqualifying copy** in headline, summary, bullet overrides, projects, `coverLetter`, or `personalNote`. Do not invent facts. **Do not tell the employer you fail their requirements** — no volunteering gaps, missing years, "not aligned with…", "I lack X from the JD", "upfront…", or apologies for fit. Present only truthful strengths; **they** judge match from what you show. Private `notes` on a version is for agent context only (e.g. what not to claim).
- **Natural angle, not JD cosplay.** Match the role by spotlighting real technical work and high skills. Do **not** bolt on awkward, over-specific closers or soft-skill lines that sound written for the posting (e.g. "Motivated to configure workspaces and guide users on Monday.com", "Strong English", "university TA…", "passionate about client enablement"). Summaries should read like a strong engineer/practitioner describing what they do well; motivation lines stay general and process/skill oriented when used at all (e.g. clearer processes, less manual work), not product- or duty-name echoes.
- **Don't let the JD's own job title become the candidate's self-description.** If your established identity (per master.yaml / knowledge base) is broader or different from the posting's title (e.g. the posting says "Backend Engineer" but you're full-stack), tailor emphasis toward the posting's theme without rewriting headline/summary/cover letter to claim the posting's narrower label as your own. Old saved CVs or informal past wording are not evidence of identity — master.yaml and your knowledge base are.
- **No YAML line breaks inside hyphenated words.** Folded `>` blocks turn newlines into spaces, so `cross-\nrepository` becomes `cross- repository` on the PDF. Keep hyphenated words on one line (`cross-repository`).
- Prefer not to put candidate home city, age, or gender in headline or summary unless the user asks for it.
- **No job posting location** in `coverLetter`, `personalNote`, or other applicant-facing copy: drop "in Raanana", "Tel Aviv on site", etc.; use company + role only.
- Employer location on an experience line (company + city) is fine — that describes the employer, not the candidate or the posting.
- Use past tense for previous roles and present tense only for current roles or general self-description that is still true.

## What tailoring is (and is not)

**Allowed:** `headline`, `summary`, `bulletOverrides` (same underlying facts, clearer emphasis for this JD), `projectOverrides`, `skillOverrides` (reorder or rephrase **only skills already in master** — do not add tools/frameworks not already listed), hide/reorder bullets and projects.

**Allowed (rare):** `experienceAdditions` + `experienceOrder` only for entries the user has explicitly confirmed and documented (e.g. thesis/academic bullets), using verified copy — not new employers.

**Not allowed:** inventing skills, years, employers, metrics, or tools; **`roleTitleOverrides`**; JD keywords that are not backed by master/knowledge base; implying a different job title than master; claiming seniority, platform, or domain experience the user does not have; **calling out misalignment** with the posting (missing reqs, wrong seniority, wrong stack) in any applicant-facing text.

## Base CVs are the wording source of truth
- **Do not rewrite headline/summary/bullets from scratch for every new JD.** Base profiles under `data/bases/` hold "winning" phrases — approved voice and structure, reused across many CVs. List `data/bases/` to see what exists locally before choosing.
- **Note:** `extends` in this codebase only ever resolves against `master.yaml` (there is no saved-CV → base → master inheritance chain in the app). "Start from a base" is a copy-and-adjust workflow for you, the agent, not a data-model feature: open the closest-matching base file, copy its `headline`/`summary`/`bulletOverrides`/`projectOverrides` text into the new `data/saved/<slug>.yaml`, and only reword the specific parts this JD needs emphasized differently. Do not invent new phrasing for parts a base already covers well.
- Pick whichever base's role family best matches the JD's core theme; if none fits well, use the user's general/default base.
- Reusing a base's wording is not "not tailoring" — it *is* the tailoring philosophy here: the base already encodes the right emphasis for that role family; a specific JD should only shift a few bullets/skills/project lines beyond that, not regenerate everything.

## Tailoring a version
1. Create or update `data/saved/<slug>.yaml` with `extends: master`.
2. Override with: `headline`, `summary`, `hiddenBulletIds`, `hiddenProjectIds`, `bulletOverrides`, `experienceBulletOrder`, `projectOrder`, `skillCategoryOrder`, `skillOverrides`, `projectOverrides`.
3. **Never set `roleTitleOverrides`.** If a printed title looks wrong, that's a master.yaml correction to confirm with the user, not a per-CV override.
4. **Optional (rare):** `experienceAdditions` + `experienceOrder` for entries the user has confirmed (e.g. thesis), using verified copy. `education` override to avoid sidebar duplication.
5. Lead with the strongest match for the target role; hide lower-priority bullets only when needed for space.
6. Keep output to one A4 page — shorten wording before hiding factual bullets.
7. Confirm with the user before dropping in-progress project features they intend to keep.
8. User can use **Save as base…** to create a new base CV or replace an existing one.

## When user sends a job description
- Read `data/master.yaml` (and the user's personal knowledge base file, if they have one) first.
- Match JD themes to existing bullet/project `tags` — **emphasize what fits**; silently omit what does not (do not narrate the omission on the CV or letter).
- Create a saved version under `data/saved/` (or BYO AI tailor in the UI).
- Rewrite headline + summary to align with the role using **only** master/knowledge-base facts.
- In `projectOverrides`, lead each description with a plain-English "what this product is" sentence (from master), then role-specific stack details.
- Tell the user what was emphasized, hidden, or reworded — not invented.
- Tell the user to click **Reload** after file edits.
- **Page fit (agents) — mandatory, not optional.** After editing `data/saved/*.yaml` or `data/bases/*.yaml`, run `npm run check-page-fit -- <version-id>` from the repo root (uses the same A4 measurement as preview). Exit `0` = in the target band; exit `1` = overflow or too tight; exit `3` = too sparse (see below). **Saying "done" while this command exits non-zero is a rule violation** — it is not the user's job to notice a bad page from a screenshot; run the check yourself before handing off. The in-app preview also shows a live banner ("Content overflows the page" / "Page looks under-filled") driven by the same measurement, so a visible banner means the same thing as a non-zero exit code. Requires `playwright` + Chromium once: `npx playwright install chromium`.
- **Page density (agents):** A good CV **fills the page** without clipping. Treat `sparePx` from the check as a density gauge on a ~1123px A4 page:
  - **`overflows: true` or `sparePx < 55`** — too long; shorten copy or hide lower-priority bullets/projects until fixed. Headless Playwright often under-measures vs the in-app preview by ~10–20px — never hand off below **55px** spare.
  - **`sparePx` 55–75** — target band: page looks full, safe bottom margin, no overflow in preview.
  - **`sparePx > 75`** — too empty; add back relevant bullets, projects, or richer (still honest) wording before handing off.
  - Iterate: run check → adjust → re-run until `overflows: false` and spare is in the **55–75px** band. **Do not hand off** until both pass.
- **If editing bullets/summary/projects doesn't move `sparePx` at all, you are editing the wrong column.** The CV layout is two columns: `.cv-sidebar` (Contact, Skills, Education) and `.cv-main` (Summary/Experience/Projects); page height is whichever column is taller. If the sidebar is taller, every edit to bullets, summary, or project text is silently a no-op on `sparePx` — you must trim or expand **Education** (detail-line count and length) or **Skills** instead. Confirm which column is the bottleneck by making one small, deliberate edit to Education and re-running the check: if `sparePx` moves, the sidebar was the constraint; if not, it is `.cv-main` (bullets/projects/summary). Do not repeatedly reword bullets when the number is stuck — check the other column first. Also note: adding/removing text only changes height when it crosses a line-wrap boundary — a shorter sentence that still wraps to the same number of lines will not change `sparePx` at all, so prefer removing/adding a whole detail line (a full `<li>`, e.g. splitting or merging an education detail item) over shaving a few words when you need fine control.

## Cover letters (optional, per saved version)
- Store on the saved YAML as `coverLetter` (plain text). Omit when unused.
- Optional `jobDescription` on the same saved YAML: the JD used for this application. Persist when creating/saving so Cover letter / AI Tailor can prefill without re-paste. Not shown on the A4 CV; strip on Save copy / promote-to-base (same as cover letter).
- Match the JD and role type using master + that saved/resolved CV; invent nothing.
- Do not list JD requirements you do not meet, compare yourself negatively to the posting, or apologize for fit — present matching strengths confidently; the reader decides alignment from truthful content only.
- Shape: greeting → short open → exactly 4 labeled bullets → close → sign-off + name.
- Hard length cap: no longer than a short half-page (~250–300 words), same scale as a typical one-page application letter.
- If the user has stated a preferred closing sentence or opener phrasing (in their knowledge base or otherwise), reuse it consistently rather than varying it per letter.
- Optional master `applicantBrief` for voice/extra context in BYO prompts (not on the A4 CV).
- If the user keeps a personal knowledge base file, read it before drafting: same honesty rules as the CV apply to cover letters.
- UI: Cover letter panel on saved CVs (copy prompt → paste → save → print). Do not put the letter on the CV PDF page.
- Optional `personalNote` on saved YAML: 2–4 sentences for email/LinkedIn/application text box; same honesty rules; save with cover letter via **Save to this CV**.
